Chapter 2: Mathematical Preliminaries

We work in a standard measure‑theoretic probability setting.

Definition 2.1 (Probability Space).
Let (\Theta, \mathcal{B}, \mu_0) be a probability space of latent world states. \Theta is a Polish space, \mathcal{B} its Borel \sigma-algebra.

Definition 2.2 (Observation Kernel).
A Markov kernel P: \Theta \times \mathcal{X} \to [0,1] from \Theta to a measurable space (\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{F}) assigns to each \theta\in\Theta a probability measure P(\cdot\mid\theta) on \mathcal{X}.

Definition 2.3 (Action and Utility).
Let (\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{G}) be a measurable space of actions. A utility function is a bounded measurable function U:\Theta\times\mathcal{A} \to \mathbb{R}. Without loss, assume 0 \le U \le M. This boundedness is a mathematical requirement for the stopping theorems; we shall architecturally enforce it in the value estimator (Section 5.4).

Definition 2.4 (History and Belief Update).
At each discrete time t=0,1,\dots, the agent receives observation X_t \in \mathcal{X}. The history is \mathcal{H}_t = \mathcal{X}^t (with \mathcal{H}_0 = \{\emptyset\}). The agent’s exact Bayesian belief after history h_t is the posterior measure

\mu_t(\cdot) = \mu_0(\cdot \mid h_t) = \frac{\int_\cdot \prod_{s=1}^t p(X_s\mid\theta) \, d\mu_0(\theta)}{\int_\Theta \prod_{s=1}^t p(X_s\mid\theta) \, d\mu_0(\theta)},

where p(\cdot\mid\theta) denotes the density of P(\cdot\mid\theta) w.r.t. a \sigma-finite base measure on \mathcal{X}.

Definition 2.5 (Policy and Stopping Time).
A policy \pi consists of a stopping time \tau (a \{\mathcal{H}_t\}-adapted random variable taking values in \mathbb{N}_0 \cup \{\infty\}) and a decision rule a_\tau that selects an action based on \mathcal{H}_\tau. The expected net utility of a policy is

\mathbb{E}\left[ U(\theta, a_\tau) - \sum_{t=0}^{\tau-1} c(\mathcal{H}_t) \right],

where c(\cdot): \mathcal{H}^* \to (0, \infty) is a measurable, history‑dependent cost function that is bounded below by some c_{\min} > 0 and above by c_{\max} < \infty. The expectation is taken over the data and the prior.

Definition 2.6 (Kullback–Leibler Divergence and Misspecification).
For two probability measures \mathbb{Q} and \mathbb{P} on a common space, the KL divergence is D_{KL}(\mathbb{Q}\|\mathbb{P}) = \int \log \frac{d\mathbb{Q}}{d\mathbb{P}} d\mathbb{Q} if \mathbb{Q}\ll\mathbb{P} and +\infty otherwise. When the true data‑generating process \mathbb{Q} is not contained in the model class \Phi, we say the agent faces model misspecification.

Definition 2.7 (Variational Approximation Gap).
In any neural implementation, exact posteriors \mu_t are replaced by variational approximations \tilde{\mu}_t. We denote the per‑step variational gap by \epsilon_t = D_{KL}(\tilde{\mu}_t \| \mu_t). The cumulative gap enters the predictive log‑loss bound as \mathbb{E}[\sum_t \epsilon_t] (Blei, Kucukelbir, & McAuliffe, 2017).

Definition 2.8 (Pinsker’s Inequality).
For any two probability measures \mu,\nu on a common space, \|\mu - \nu\|_{TV} \le \sqrt{\frac{1}{2} D_{KL}(\mu \| \nu)}. This inequality will be central in bounding the effect of belief approximations on decision quality.

