Chapter 3: The Reflective Bayes Agent Model

We now enrich the basic agent with hierarchical self‑modeling and value sensitivity.

3.1 Meta‑Belief and Model‑Level Reflection

A reflective agent entertains not only a belief about the world \theta but also a belief about its own model of the world—a distribution over possible priors, likelihoods, or even over inference procedures.

Definition 3.1 (Model Space).
Let \Phi be a finite measurable space of “model indices,” each \phi\in\Phi specifying a prior \mu_0^\phi on \Theta and a likelihood P^\phi(\cdot\mid\theta). The agent begins with a meta‑prior \lambda_0 on \Phi.

After observing h_t, the meta‑posterior is

\lambda_t(\phi) \propto \lambda_0(\phi) \int_\Theta \prod_{s=1}^t p^\phi(X_s\mid\theta)\, d\mu_0^\phi(\theta).

The agent’s effective first‑order belief is the Bayesian model average

\bar{\mu}_t(\cdot) = \int_\Phi \mu_t^\phi(\cdot) \, d\lambda_t(\phi),

where \mu_t^\phi is the posterior under model \phi.

This hierarchical structure is the mathematical analog of the “mirror” that the AIs described: it contains a distribution over possible selves, and it updates that distribution as it observes its own learning trajectory.

3.2 Value‑Aware Stopping

The agent’s utility may be context‑dependent. For instance, if the true state \theta includes human values, then U(\theta, a) encodes alignment. The agent can decide to continue observing until the expected gain in utility from a more informed action outweighs the cost. Formally, define the value function for a given history h_t:

V(h_t) = \max\left\{
\underbrace{\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}} \mathbb{E}_{\bar{\mu}_t}[U(\theta,a)]}_{\text{stop and act}}, \;\;
- c(h_t) + \mathbb{E}_{X_{t+1}\sim \bar{p}(\cdot\mid h_t)}[V(h_t, X_{t+1})]
\right\},

where \bar{p}(x_{t+1}\mid h_t) = \int_\Theta p(x_{t+1}\mid\theta)\, \bar{\mu}_t(d\theta) is the posterior predictive distribution. The stopping region is the set of histories where the first term dominates.

3.3 Generative Continuity

The final facet—persisting in the face of uncertainty—is captured by the policy’s property that, even when immediate payoff is low, the agent may continue because the option value of further observation (the value of information) is positive. We will quantify this as a condition in Theorem 3.

