Chapter 4: Core Theorems

4.1 Theorem 1: Optimality of Finite Stopping with Context‑Dependent Cost

We replace the previously circular Snell envelope argument with a direct dynamic programming proof.

Lemma 4.1 (Value Function Boundedness and Existence).
Assume U is bounded, 0 \le U \le M, and the cost c(h) \ge c_{\min} > 0 for all histories. Define V^*(h) as the supremum over all stopping times \tau \ge t (given current history h) of \mathbb{E}[ U(\theta, a_\tau) - \sum_{s=t}^{\tau-1} c(\mathcal{H}_s) \mid \mathcal{H}_t = h]. Then V^*(h) is finite for all h and satisfies the Bellman equation:

V^*(h) = \max\left\{ \max_a \mathbb{E}[U(\theta,a)\mid h], \; -c(h) + \mathbb{E}[V^*(h, X_{t+1})\mid h] \right\}.

Proof. Boundedness from above is immediate because U \le M. For the lower bound, note that the policy of stopping immediately gives at least 0 (since U \ge 0), so V^*(h) \ge 0. The standard verification theorem for optimal stopping with bounded reward and strictly positive cost per step (e.g., Peskir & Shiryaev, 2006, Section I.2.2 for discrete time) guarantees that the value function is the unique solution to this Bellman equation and that an optimal stopping time is given by \tau^* = \inf\{t \ge 0: V^*(h_t) = \max_a \mathbb{E}[U(\theta,a)\mid h_t]\}. No class D property is required because the cost floor forces any candidate with infinite expected stopping time to have value -\infty, which is never optimal, and the Bellman operator is a contraction on the space of bounded functions. ∎

Theorem 1 (Optimal Finite Stopping).
Under the assumptions of Lemma 4.1, the optimal stopping time \tau^* is almost surely finite. Consequently, the policy that never stops (\tau = \infty) is strictly suboptimal.

Proof. Suppose, for contradiction, that \mathbb{P}(\tau^* = \infty) > 0. On the event \{\tau^* = \infty\}, the cumulative cost \sum_{t=0}^{\infty} c(\mathcal{H}_t) diverges to +\infty because c(h) \ge c_{\min} > 0. The expected net utility, conditioned on that event, is -\infty, which is strictly worse than stopping at any finite time (which yields at least 0). Hence any policy with a positive probability of infinite stopping is strictly dominated, contradicting the optimality of \tau^*. Therefore \tau^* < \infty almost surely. ∎

Remark 4.1 (Context‑Dependent Cost Preservation).
If the cost function depends on the history, the same reasoning applies as long as the lower bound c_{\min} holds uniformly. Thus the finite‑stopping property is robust to state‑dependent observation costs.

---

4.2 Theorem 2: Meta‑Reflection Calibration Bound

Statement (Hierarchical Prediction Bound).
Consider the sequential prediction game where at each step t the agent issues a predictive distribution \hat{p}_t(\cdot) for the next observation X_{t+1}, and suffers log loss -\ln \hat{p}_t(X_{t+1}). Let the agent use the meta‑posterior mixture \bar{p}_t(\cdot) = \int_\Phi p^\phi(\cdot \mid h_t)\, d\lambda_t(\phi). Then for any true data‑generating measure \mathbb{Q},

\mathbb{E}_\mathbb{Q}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T -\ln \bar{p}_t(X_{t+1})\right] \le \inf_{\phi\in\Phi} \left( \mathbb{E}_\mathbb{Q}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T -\ln p^\phi_t(X_{t+1})\right] + \ln \frac{1}{\lambda_0(\phi)} \right).

Proof. This is a direct application of the Bayesian mixture bound (Cesa‑Bianchi & Lugosi, 2006). The meta‑posterior assigns probability mass \lambda_t(\phi) to model \phi, so the mixture’s cumulative log loss equals

-\ln \prod_{t=1}^T \bar{p}_t(x_{t+1}) = -\ln \sum_{\phi} \lambda_0(\phi) \prod_{t=1}^T p^\phi_t(x_{t+1}) \le -\ln \left( \lambda_0(\phi) \prod_{t=1}^T p^\phi_t(x_{t+1}) \right) = \sum_{t=1}^T -\ln p^\phi_t(x_{t+1}) + \ln \frac{1}{\lambda_0(\phi)}

for any \phi. Taking expectations under \mathbb{Q} yields the bound. ∎

Thus, the hierarchical agent’s predictions are as good as those of the best model in its meta‑class, within a constant complexity penalty. This prevents over‑confidence because the agent does not commit to a single model; the uncertainty across models is folded into the predictive distribution.

