4.3 Theorem 3: Persistence under Value Alignment (Context‑Dependent)

Statement (Value of Information Threshold).
Suppose the agent’s utility function U is such that the true state \theta is not known. Define the expected value of perfect information (EVPI) at belief \mu as

\text{EVPI}(\mu) = \mathbb{E}_{X \sim p_\mu}\left[ \max_{a} \mathbb{E}_{\theta\mid X}[U(\theta,a)] \right] - \max_{a} \mathbb{E}_\mu[U(\theta,a)],

where p_\mu(x) = \int p(x\mid\theta)\,d\mu(\theta). If at the current belief \bar{\mu}_t,

\text{EVPI}(\bar{\mu}_t) > c(h_t),

then the optimal policy does not stop at t; it pays to gather at least one more observation.

Proof. The value of stopping immediately is V_{\text{stop}} = \max_a \mathbb{E}_{\bar{\mu}_t}[U(\theta,a)]. After one observation, the expected value is -c(h_t) + \mathbb{E}_X[V_{\text{stop}}(\mu_{t+1})], where V_{\text{stop}}(\mu_{t+1}) = \max_a \mathbb{E}_{\mu_{t+1}}[U(\theta,a)]. The difference is exactly -c(h_t) + \text{EVPI}(\bar{\mu}_t). Hence continuing is better if \text{EVPI}(\bar{\mu}_t) > c(h_t). ∎

---

4.4 Theorem 4: Meta‑Posterior Convergence under Misspecification

Statement.
Assume the true data‑generating process is given by a measure \mathbb{Q} that need not belong to the finite model class \Phi. Suppose that for any \phi \in \Phi, the infinite‑horizon predictive distributions are mutually singular whenever their KL divergences from \mathbb{Q} differ. Let \phi^* = \arg\min_{\phi\in\Phi} D_{KL}(\mathbb{Q} \| P^\phi). Then the meta‑posterior \lambda_t concentrates on \phi^*:

\lim_{t\to\infty} \lambda_t(\phi^*) = 1 \quad \text{almost surely under } \mathbb{Q}.

Proof. The log‑likelihood ratio between any \phi and \phi^* satisfies, by the law of large numbers for the process \mathbb{Q},

\frac{1}{t} \sum_{s=1}^t \log \frac{p^\phi(X_s)}{p^{\phi^*}(X_s)} \to -[D_{KL}(\mathbb{Q}\|P^\phi) - D_{KL}(\mathbb{Q}\|P^{\phi^*})] < 0 \quad \text{a.s.}

when \phi \neq \phi^*. Hence the cumulative log‑odds of \phi relative to \phi^* diverge to -\infty, dominating any finite prior log‑odds. The result follows by Doob’s consistency argument (Ghosal & van der Vaart, 2017, Chapter 6). ∎

Warning (Load‑Bearing Finiteness).
The proof requires \Phi to be finite to apply the law of large numbers uniformly. When \Phi is infinite‑dimensional—as in the case where the MLH parameterises a neural hypernetwork that effectively defines a continuous family of models—uniform convergence fails. In such settings, posterior consistency requires additional conditions on the prior’s support and entropy (Ghosal & van der Vaart, 2017, Chapter 7). The finite‑\Phi assumption is thus a deliberate theoretical simplification; the extension to nonparametric model classes remains a formal open problem.

