4.5 Approximation Gap and the Cold Posterior Effect

In any neural implementation, exact posteriors \mu_t and meta‑posteriors \lambda_t are replaced by variational approximations \tilde{\mu}_t. Let \epsilon_t = D_{KL}(\tilde{\mu}_t \| \mu_t) denote the per‑step variational gap. A standard consequence of the chain rule for KL divergence is that the cumulative gap in predictive log‑loss satisfies

\mathbb{E}_\mathbb{Q}\left[ \sum_{t=1}^T -\ln \tilde{p}_t(X_{t+1}) \right] \le \mathbb{E}_\mathbb{Q}\left[ \sum_{t=1}^T -\ln \bar{p}_t(X_{t+1}) \right] + \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{t=1}^T \epsilon_t \right],

where \tilde{p}_t is the approximate predictive distribution. This bound is standard in variational inference (Blei et al., 2017).

When the approximation is further distorted by a temperature parameter \beta (the cold posterior effect), the effective belief becomes \tilde{\mu}_t^{(\beta)}, obtained by multiplying the log‑likelihood by \beta during inference. The cold posterior literature shows that \beta > 1 sharpens the posterior (colder) and \beta < 1 makes it wider (warmer) (Wenzel et al., 2020; Aitchison, 2020). We denote by q_\beta the tempered variational posterior and by q_1 the standard (\beta=1) variational posterior. The distortion introduced by temperature is measured by \sigma_t = D_{KL}(q_\beta(z_t) \| q_1(z_t)), the KL divergence between the tempered and untempered approximate posteriors at step t. This \sigma_t captures the prior–likelihood conflict induced by the temperature.

Lemma 4.2 (EVPI Sensitivity to Belief Perturbation).
Let \mu and \nu be two beliefs over \Theta. Then, assuming a finite action space and bounded utility U \in [0,M],

|\text{EVPI}(\mu) - \text{EVPI}(\nu)| \le 3M \sqrt{\frac{1}{2} D_{KL}(\mu \| \nu)}.

Proof. Let V(\mu) = \max_a \mathbb{E}_\mu[U(\theta,a)]. For any two beliefs, |V(\mu)-V(\nu)| \le M \|\mu-\nu\|_{TV} \le M \sqrt{\frac{1}{2} D_{KL}(\mu\|\nu)} by Pinsker’s inequality. Now,

\text{EVPI}(\mu) = \mathbb{E}_{X\sim p_\mu}[ V(\mu_X) ] - V(\mu),

where \mu_X is the posterior after observing X. Then

\begin{aligned}
|\text{EVPI}(\mu)-\text{EVPI}(\nu)|
&\le \mathbb{E}_{p_\mu}\bigl[|V(\mu_X)-V(\nu_X)|\bigr]
+ \bigl|\mathbb{E}_{p_\mu}[V(\nu_X)] - \mathbb{E}_{p_\nu}[V(\nu_X)]\bigr|
+ |V(\mu)-V(\nu)|.
\end{aligned}

The first term is bounded by M \mathbb{E}_{p_\mu}\bigl[ \sqrt{\frac{1}{2} D_{KL}(\mu_X\|\nu_X)} \bigr]
\le M \sqrt{\frac{1}{2} \mathbb{E}_{p_\mu}[ D_{KL}(\mu_X\|\nu_X) ]}
= M \sqrt{\frac{1}{2} D_{KL}(\mu\|\nu)}, because \mathbb{E}_{p_\mu}[ D_{KL}(\mu_X\|\nu_X) ] = D_{KL}(\mu\|\nu) when the likelihood is the same. The second term is bounded by M \|p_\mu - p_\nu\|_{TV} \le M \sqrt{\frac{1}{2} D_{KL}(\mu\|\nu)}. The third term is bounded by M \sqrt{\frac{1}{2} D_{KL}(\mu\|\nu)}. Summing gives the constant 3M. ∎

Applying Lemma 4.2 to \mu = \mu_t (true posterior) and \nu = \tilde{\mu}_t^{(\beta)} (tempered variational approximation) yields

|\text{EVPI}(\tilde{\mu}_t^{(\beta)}) - \text{EVPI}(\mu_t)| \le 3M \sqrt{\frac{1}{2} D_{KL}(\tilde{\mu}_t^{(\beta)} \| \mu_t)}.

The KL divergence inside the square root can be decomposed into the variational gap \epsilon_t plus the temperature‑induced shift. Using the chain rule for KL and the definition of \sigma_t, one obtains

D_{KL}(\tilde{\mu}_t^{(\beta)} \| \mu_t) \le \epsilon_t + |\beta-1| \cdot \sigma_t + O(|\beta-1|^2).

Consequently, the stopping regret—the difference in expected utility between the exact optimal policy and the approximate policy—satisfies

\mathbb{E}[\text{stopping regret}] \le \text{const} \cdot \sum_t \left( \sqrt{\epsilon_t} + \sqrt{|\beta-1| \cdot \sigma_t} \right),

where the constant depends on M and the Lipschitz constant. This bound shows that the stopping controller’s performance degrades gracefully as long as the temperature is learned to calibrate the predictive distribution, which we enforce via a PAC‑Bayesian meta‑learning objective (Section 5.5). The specific PAC‑Bayes bound for tempered posteriors follows Alquier et al. (2016), who provide risk certificates for Gibbs posteriors under model misspecification.

