Chapter 5: Architecture of a Reflective Intelligence System

We now sketch a concrete computational architecture that realises the Reflective Bayes Agent. The design is hybrid neural‑probabilistic, stated in terms of differentiable modules with clear theoretical counterparts. The architecture respects the theoretical bounds of Chapter 4 and incorporates the practical safeguards discussed therein.

5.1 Overview

The system consists of four main modules:

1. Bayesian World Model (BWM) – a variational recurrent neural network that maintains an approximate belief over latent variables.
2. Meta‑Learning Hypernetwork (MLH) – a neural network that outputs the parameters of the meta‑prior \lambda_0 and, if needed, the structure of the BWM, implementing hierarchical meta‑cognition.
3. Value Estimator (VE) – a network that approximates Q(\mu, a) = \mathbb{E}_\mu[U(\theta,a)] for different actions, with an architectural normalisation guaranteeing bounded outputs.
4. Stopping Controller (SC) – a decision module that compares the estimated EVPI with a context‑dependent cost, deciding whether to stop or to continue internal deliberation.

5.2 Bayesian World Model and Approximate Inference

The core belief engine is a variational recurrent neural network (VRNN; Chung et al., 2015). At each step t, the model encodes the current observation x_t into a stochastic latent variable z_t, with a recurrent state h_t summarising the past. The generative model is:

p(x_t, z_t \mid h_{t-1}) = p(z_t \mid h_{t-1})\, p(x_t \mid z_t, h_{t-1}),

and the inference network provides an approximate posterior q(z_t \mid x_t, h_{t-1}). The belief \mu_t over the world’s hidden state is maintained as the distribution of z_t, h_t.

We explicitly adopt a mean‑field variational approximation. The gap between the exact posterior and the variational distribution is monitored via the variational free energy (ELBO). To address the cold posterior effect, a learnable temperature parameter \beta is applied to the likelihood term during training. Correct formulation: To achieve a colder (sharper) posterior, the log‑likelihood is multiplied by \beta > 1; to smooth it, \beta < 1. Thus the tempered ELBO is

\mathcal{L}_{\text{BWM}} = \beta \cdot \mathbb{E}_{q} \left[ \ln p(x_t \mid z_t, h_{t-1}) \right] - D_{KL}(q(z_t) \| p(z_t \mid h_{t-1})),

where \beta is optimised jointly with the other parameters (Wenzel et al., 2020; Aitchison, 2020). The temperature is constrained to a sensible range (e.g., \beta \in [0.1, 10]) and guided by a PAC‑Bayesian bound on the predictive risk (Alquier et al., 2016).

5.3 Meta‑Learning Hypernetwork

The MLH is a neural network \psi that, conditioned on a context window of recent experience (e.g., the agent’s own prediction errors and free energy values), outputs the parameters of the prior over latent variables, as well as the initial meta‑prior distribution over a discrete set of candidate model configurations. In a discrete model class with K members, the MLH outputs logits \ell_\phi, defining \lambda_0(\phi) \propto \exp(\ell_\phi).

Computational Complexity and Amortization.
The meta‑posterior update requires marginalising over all K models, costing O(K \cdot d) per step where d is the evidence evaluation cost. To scale to large K, we propose to use amortised inference: a hypernetwork directly outputs \lambda_t(\phi) given a summary of the history. The per‑step cost then becomes O(d_{\text{amort}}), independent of K. The amortisation error can be bounded by the KL divergence between the true and amortised meta‑posterior; see Cremer et al. (2018) for an explicit analysis of inference suboptimality in variational autoencoders, and Kim et al. (2018) for semi‑amortized bounds. We assume the amortisation error is controlled such that the meta‑reflection bound (Theorem 2) holds up to a small additive constant.

5.4 Value Estimator and Stopping Controller

Norm‑Bounded Value Estimator.
The VE must produce outputs in [0, M] to satisfy the bounded‑utility premise of Theorems 1 and 3. We architecturally enforce this by applying a scaled logistic function to the final layer: \text{VE}(\text{belief\_state}, a) = M \cdot \sigma(\text{NN}(\text{belief\_state})_a). This guarantees that the estimated U is always within [0,M], directly satisfying the theoretical requirement.

Context‑Dependent Cost Module.
The stopping controller does not use a single fixed c. Instead, the cost is output by a sub‑network C_\psi(h_t) that is trained to predict the actual compute cost (e.g., measured in energy or time) of continuing deliberation. To keep the theoretical finite‑stopping guarantee, we enforce c(h_t) \ge c_{\min} > 0 via a softplus transformation with a floor:

c(h_t) = c_{\min} + \text{softplus}(C_\psi(h_t)).

Warm‑Start Note.
During early training, when C_\psi outputs arbitrary values, the floor may dominate, effectively setting c(h_t) \approx c_{\min} for all histories and collapsing the context‑dependence. To avoid this degenerate local minimum, we propose a training schedule: for the first k steps, c(h_t) is fixed to a default value \bar{c} estimated from average compute cost; thereafter, C_\psi is gradually annealed in, with the floor lowered to c_{\min} over subsequent epochs. This curriculum ensures that the context‑dependent component learns meaningful variations before taking full control.

Stopping Rule.
The SC computes the value of stopping now: V_{\text{stop}} = \max_a \text{VE}(\bar{\mu}_t, a). It estimates EVPI via Monte Carlo roll‑outs in the BWM (or via a learned approximation). If \text{EVPI}(\bar{\mu}_t) > c(h_t), the agent continues internal deliberation; otherwise, it outputs the action a^* = \arg\max_a \text{VE}(\bar{\mu}_t, a).

5.5 Training Objective

The full system is trained end‑to‑end on a combined loss:

\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{BWM}} + \alpha \mathcal{L}_{\text{meta}} + \gamma \mathcal{L}_{\text{RL}},

where \mathcal{L}_{\text{BWM}} is the tempered ELBO, \mathcal{L}_{\text{meta}} is the meta‑posterior log loss (encouraging calibration as in Theorem 2), and \mathcal{L}_{\text{RL}} is a utility‑weighted reinforcement learning objective for the VE and SC, with reward U(\theta, a) - \sum c(h_t). The temperature \beta and cost network C_\psi are trained jointly. The PAC‑Bayesian objective for \beta is based on the bound from Alquier et al. (2016) (see also Germain et al., 2016 for the general PAC‑Bayes framework).

