Chapter 6: Multi‑Agent Reflective Intelligence

The reflective self‑model becomes even more powerful when multiple agents share their mirrors. We extend the framework to a collective setting.

6.1 Two‑Agent Reflective System

Consider two RBA agents, indexed by i=1,2, each with its own meta‑model class \Phi_i, prior over world states \mu_0^{(i)}, and private observation streams X_{1,t}, X_{2,t}. The agents share a common meta‑posterior \lambda_t over the joint model class \Phi = \Phi_1 \times \Phi_2 (or over a subset of shared hypotheses). After each agent updates its local belief, it transmits its model evidence (marginal likelihood) for each \phi to the other. The shared meta‑posterior is then

\lambda_t(\phi) \propto \lambda_0(\phi) \prod_{i=1}^2 \int \prod_{s=1}^t p^{\phi_i}(X_{i,s}\mid\theta)\, d\mu_0^{\phi_i}(\theta).

The agents then act using their own effective beliefs \bar{\mu}_t^{(i)}, which now incorporate the other’s data.

6.2 Collective Convergence Theorem

Theorem 5 (Collective Meta‑Reflection Acceleration).
Let the joint process \{(X_{1,t}, X_{2,t})\}_{t\ge 1} be strictly stationary and ergodic. Assume the true data‑generating measure \mathbb{Q} factorises conditionally on \theta: (X_{1,t} \perp X_{2,t}) \mid \theta, and that for each agent i and each model \phi \in \Phi, the per‑step log‑likelihood ratios relative to the best model \phi^* satisfy a uniform law of large numbers. Suppose further that the shared meta‑prior \lambda_0 has full support. Then the shared meta‑posterior \lambda_t concentrates on \phi^* at a rate

-\frac{1}{t} \ln \lambda_t(\phi^*) \le \frac{1}{t} \ln \frac{1}{\lambda_0(\phi^*)} + o(1),

and for any \phi \neq \phi^*,

\frac{1}{t} \ln \frac{\lambda_t(\phi)}{\lambda_t(\phi^*)} \to -D_\phi < 0,

where D_\phi = D_{KL}(\mathbb{Q}^{(1)}\|P^{\phi}) + D_{KL}(\mathbb{Q}^{(2)}\|P^{\phi}) - 2 D_{KL}(\mathbb{Q}\|P^{\phi^*}) (with marginals). Under conditional independence, the cumulative log‑evidence is the sum of individual log‑evidences, so the posterior odds of a false model decay at double the single‑agent rate.

Proof Sketch. By the product‑space ergodic theorem, the time average of the joint log‑likelihood ratio converges almost surely to the sum of individual expected divergences. For two stationary, mixing Markov chains with unique invariant measures—the typical output of a VRNN—the joint process is indeed ergodic under the product transformation (Vidyasagar, 2003, Chapter 3). The remaining argument mirrors Theorem 4, applied to the product measure. The acceleration factor of 2 is exact under independence.

Corollary (Degradation under Correlation).
If observations are correlated with correlation coefficient \rho = \mathbb{E}[\text{Cov}(\log p^\phi, \log p^{\phi^*})] / (\sigma_\phi \sigma_{\phi^*}), the effective drift becomes D_\phi \cdot (2 - \rho), reducing the acceleration. A formal bound follows from mixing sequence concentration inequalities (Rio, 2017, Theorem 2.1). ∎

Remark. The product ergodicity condition may fail if the agents share a common latent variable whose trajectory creates long‑range dependence across streams. The theorem therefore assumes that any shared latent structure is sufficiently mixing; in the VRNN setting, this holds when the latent dynamics are ergodic.

