Chapter 7: Relation to Active Inference

The Reflective Bayes Agent independently reconstructs many elements of active inference, a process theory derived from the free energy principle (Friston, 2010). A recent formal synthesis (Smith et al., 2025) proves equivalences between active inference and Bayesian decision theory, optimal control, and resource rationality. We now position the RBA explicitly within this literature, highlighting points of convergence and novel contribution.

7.1 Formal Mapping

Active inference agents minimise a variational free energy functional over beliefs and policies. In the fully observed limit, this corresponds to exact Bayesian filtering and optimal decision‑making. The RBA’s belief updating (Section 3.1) is exactly the expectation step of variational Bayes; the meta‑posterior extends this to model comparison, corresponding to a hierarchical active inference agent that maintains beliefs over model structures (Friston et al., 2016). The stopping controller can be seen as a threshold on the expected free energy (EFE) of future policies: continuing observation reduces epistemic uncertainty, which is a component of EFE. Theorem 3 thus provides an explicit cost‑benefit condition for epistemic action that is consistent with, but sharper than, the generic EFE minimisation.

7.2 Novel Contributions Relative to Active Inference

The existing active inference literature already contains hierarchical “sophisticated” agents that maintain beliefs about model structure and perform deep tree search over belief states (Friston et al., 2020). Therefore, the claim of hierarchical meta‑cognition as a standalone novelty is overstated. The genuine novelty of the RBA lies in the cost‑sensitive, threshold‑based stopping rule—Theorems 1 and 3 together provide a computable, single‑step decision condition, as opposed to the policy‑distribution‑over‑all‑future‑steps approach of active inference. This makes the stopping decision analytically transparent and directly implementable as a comparison between EVPI and context‑dependent cost, a contribution not present in the EFE minimisation framework. Additionally, the explicit incorporation of a learnable observation cost and the formal treatment of the cold posterior effect within the stopping module are unique to this thesis.

