Appendix A: Mathematical Foundations

A.1 Probability Theory and Measure-Theoretic Setup

We work on a probability space (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P}). All random variables are measurable functions from \Omega to a measurable state space. We assume that \Theta (latent world states) is a Polish space equipped with its Borel \sigma-algebra \mathcal{B}(\Theta). The prior \mu_0 is a probability measure on (\Theta, \mathcal{B}(\Theta)).

Definition A.1 (Markov Kernel). A mapping P: \Theta \times \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{X}) \to [0,1] is a Markov kernel from \Theta to a measurable space (\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{F}) if (i) for each \theta \in \Theta, P(\cdot \mid \theta) is a probability measure on \mathcal{F}, and (ii) for each A \in \mathcal{F}, the map \theta \mapsto P(A \mid \theta) is \mathcal{B}(\Theta)-measurable.

Definition A.2 (Bayesian Posterior). Given a prior \mu_0 and observations x_{1:t} = (x_1,\dots,x_t), the posterior \mu_t is the probability measure defined by

\mu_t(B) = \frac{\int_B \prod_{s=1}^t p(x_s \mid \theta) \, d\mu_0(\theta)}{\int_\Theta \prod_{s=1}^t p(x_s \mid \theta) \, d\mu_0(\theta)}, \quad B \in \mathcal{B}(\Theta),

where p(x \mid \theta) is the density of the observation kernel with respect to some \sigma-finite base measure \nu on \mathcal{X} (assumed to exist).

A.2 Stochastic Processes and Filtrations

The agent’s observations are modeled as a stochastic process \{X_t\}_{t \ge 1} on (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P}). The natural filtration is \mathcal{F}_t = \sigma(X_1,\dots,X_t) for t \ge 1, with \mathcal{F}_0 = \{\emptyset,\Omega\}. A stopping time \tau is a random variable taking values in \mathbb{N}_0 \cup \{\infty\} such that \{\tau \le t\} \in \mathcal{F}_t for all t.

Definition A.3 (Adapted Process). A process \{Y_t\} is adapted to \{\mathcal{F}_t\} if Y_t is \mathcal{F}_t-measurable for each t.

A.3 Stopping Times and Optimal Stopping Theory

We rely on the theory of optimal stopping in discrete time. Let \{Y_t\} be an adapted reward process. The Snell envelope \{S_t\} is the smallest supermartingale dominating Y_t:

S_t = \operatorname{ess\,sup}_{\tau \ge t} \mathbb{E}[Y_\tau \mid \mathcal{F}_t],

where the supremum is taken over all stopping times \tau \ge t. The optimal stopping time is \tau^* = \inf\{t \ge 0 : S_t = Y_t\}, provided the process is of class D and appropriate integrability holds. In the dissertation, we avoid heavy reliance on Snell theory by using direct dynamic programming arguments (Lemma 4.1) that do not require class D; we instead use the boundedness of the reward and a positive cost floor to prove that any optimal stopping time is almost surely finite.

A.4 Bayesian Inference and Posterior Convergence

The posterior distribution under model \phi evolves according to Bayes’ rule. Let \mathbb{Q} be the true data-generating measure. The Kullback-Leibler divergence between \mathbb{Q} and a model P^\phi is

D_{KL}(\mathbb{Q} \| P^\phi) = \mathbb{E}_\mathbb{Q}\left[ \log \frac{d\mathbb{Q}}{dP^\phi} \right],

if \mathbb{Q} \ll P^\phi and +\infty otherwise. The law of large numbers for \mathbb{Q} states that

\frac{1}{t} \sum_{s=1}^t \log \frac{p^{\phi}(X_s)}{p^{\phi^*}(X_s)} \to -(D_{KL}(\mathbb{Q}\|P^\phi) - D_{KL}(\mathbb{Q}\|P^{\phi^*})) \quad \mathbb{Q}\text{-a.s.}

This forms the basis for the proof of Theorem 4.

A.5 Variational Inference and KL Divergence

Variational inference approximates the true posterior \mu_t by a distribution q_t from a tractable family \mathcal{Q}. The standard objective is the evidence lower bound (ELBO):

\mathcal{L}(q) = \mathbb{E}_{q}[\log p(x \mid \theta)] - D_{KL}(q \| \mu_0).

The gap between the true posterior and the variational approximation is D_{KL}(q \| \mu_t). The chain rule for KL divergence yields the cumulative bound used in Section 4.5.

A.6 Pinsker’s Inequality and Lipschitz Bounds

Lemma A.1 (Pinsker’s Inequality). For any two probability measures \mu, \nu on a measurable space,

\|\mu - \nu\|_{TV} \le \sqrt{\frac{1}{2} D_{KL}(\mu \| \nu)}.

We use this to translate KL gaps into total variation distances, which then control expected utility differences.

A.7 Ergodic Theory and Mixing

A stationary process \{X_t\} is ergodic if for every invariant set A, \mathbb{P}(A) \in \{0,1\}. The Birkhoff ergodic theorem guarantees that time averages converge to expectations. For product spaces, if \{X_t^{(1)}\} and \{X_t^{(2)}\} are independent and ergodic, then the joint process \{(X_t^{(1)}, X_t^{(2)})\} is ergodic under the product measure. In the multi-agent setting, conditional independence given \theta ensures this property for the likelihood-ratio process. For correlated observations, we need mixing conditions (e.g., \rho-mixing) to bound the rate degradation (Rio, 2017).

A.8 Banach Fixed-Point Theorem

Theorem A.1 (Banach). Let (X, d) be a complete metric space and T: X \to X a contraction mapping, i.e., there exists \kappa \in [0,1) such that d(T(x), T(y)) \le \kappa d(x,y) for all x,y \in X. Then T has a unique fixed point x^* \in X, and for any x_0 \in X, the sequence x_{n+1} = T(x_n) converges to x^*.

We apply this to the IRL-based meta-value dynamical system under finite-dimensional parametrization (Section 8.2).

