Appendix B: Detailed Proofs

B.1 Proof of Lemma 4.1 (Value Function Boundedness and Existence)

Statement. Assume U: \Theta \times \mathcal{A} \to [0,M] is bounded and the observation cost satisfies c(h) \ge c_{\min} > 0 for all histories h. Define the value function

V^*(h) = \sup_{\tau \ge t} \mathbb{E}\left[ U(\theta, a_\tau) - \sum_{s=t}^{\tau-1} c(\mathcal{H}_s) \,\big|\, \mathcal{H}_t = h \right],

where the supremum is over all stopping times \tau \ge t and decisions a_\tau measurable with respect to \mathcal{H}_\tau. Then:
(i) V^*(h) is finite for all h, with 0 \le V^*(h) \le M.
(ii) V^* satisfies the Bellman equation

V^*(h) = \max\left\{ \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \mathbb{E}[U(\theta,a) \mid h], \; -c(h) + \mathbb{E}[V^*(h, X_{t+1}) \mid h] \right\}.

Proof.
Upper bound: For any policy, U(\theta,a_\tau) \le M, so

V^*(h) \le \sup_{\tau} \mathbb{E}[ M \mid h] = M.

Lower bound: The policy that stops immediately and chooses action a_0 yields utility \mathbb{E}[U(\theta,a_0) \mid h] \ge 0 because U \ge 0. Hence V^*(h) \ge 0.

Now we verify the Bellman equation. For any stopping time \tau \ge t, we can write the expected net reward as

J(\tau) = \mathbb{E}\left[ \mathbf{1}_{\{\tau = t\}} U(\theta,a_t) + \mathbf{1}_{\{\tau > t\}} \bigl( -c(h) + R(h, X_{t+1},\tau) \bigr) \mid h \right],

where R(h, X_{t+1},\tau) is the future net reward from time t+1 onward. Taking supremum over \tau and a_t yields the right-hand side of the Bellman equation. Standard dynamic programming results for optimal stopping with bounded rewards and strictly positive costs (see, e.g., Peskir & Shiryaev, 2006, Chapter I, Section 2.2 for the discrete-time case) guarantee that V^* is the unique solution to this equation, and an optimal stopping time exists and is given by \tau^* = \inf\{t \ge 0: V^*(h_t) = \max_a \mathbb{E}[U(\theta,a) \mid h_t]\}. No class D condition is needed because the reward is bounded and the cost is strictly positive, making the Bellman operator a contraction on the Banach space of bounded functions. ∎

B.2 Proof of Theorem 1 (Optimal Finite Stopping)

Statement. Under the assumptions of Lemma 4.1, the optimal stopping time \tau^* is almost surely finite. The policy that never stops (\tau = \infty) is strictly suboptimal.

Proof. Suppose, for contradiction, that \mathbb{P}(\tau^* = \infty) > 0. On the event \{\tau^* = \infty\}, the cumulative cost \sum_{t=0}^{\tau^*-1} c(\mathcal{H}_t) is infinite because c(\mathcal{H}_t) \ge c_{\min} > 0 for all t. Thus the conditional expected net reward on that event is -\infty. Therefore,

\mathbb{E}[ U(\theta,a_{\tau^*}) - \sum_{t=0}^{\tau^*-1} c(\mathcal{H}_t) ] \le \mathbb{P}(\tau^* = \infty) \cdot (-\infty) + \mathbb{P}(\tau^* < \infty) \cdot M = -\infty,

which is strictly less than the value of stopping at time 0, which is at least 0. Hence \tau^* cannot be optimal if it has a positive probability of being infinite. Thus \mathbb{P}(\tau^* < \infty) = 1. ∎

B.3 Proof of Theorem 2 (Meta‑Reflection Calibration Bound)

Statement. For any true data-generating measure \mathbb{Q} and any meta-prior \lambda_0 on a finite model class \Phi,

\mathbb{E}_\mathbb{Q}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T -\ln \bar{p}_t(X_{t+1})\right] \le \inf_{\phi\in\Phi} \left( \mathbb{E}_\mathbb{Q}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T -\ln p^\phi_t(X_{t+1})\right] + \ln \frac{1}{\lambda_0(\phi)} \right).

Proof. The predictive distribution under model average is \bar{p}_t(x) = \sum_{\phi} \lambda_t(\phi) p^\phi_t(x). The cumulative log loss up to time T satisfies

-\ln \prod_{t=1}^T \bar{p}_t(x_{t+1}) = -\ln \sum_{\phi} \lambda_0(\phi) \prod_{t=1}^T p^\phi_t(x_{t+1}) \le -\ln \left( \lambda_0(\phi) \prod_{t=1}^T p^\phi_t(x_{t+1}) \right),

for any fixed \phi \in \Phi. The inequality holds because the sum over all models is at least the term corresponding to \phi. Taking -\ln and applying monotonicity gives

-\ln \prod_{t=1}^T \bar{p}_t(x_{t+1}) \le \sum_{t=1}^T -\ln p^\phi_t(x_{t+1}) + \ln \frac{1}{\lambda_0(\phi)}.

Taking expectation with respect to \mathbb{Q} yields the claimed bound. ∎

B.4 Proof of Theorem 3 (Persistence Condition)

Statement. If at the current belief \bar{\mu}_t the expected value of perfect information exceeds the observation cost, i.e., \text{EVPI}(\bar{\mu}_t) > c(h_t), then the optimal policy does not stop at t.

Proof. The value of stopping immediately is V_{\text{stop}} = \max_a \mathbb{E}_{\bar{\mu}_t}[U(\theta,a)]. If the agent takes one more observation X_{t+1} and then acts optimally, the expected value conditioned on h_t is

- c(h_t) + \mathbb{E}_{X_{t+1}\sim \bar{p}(\cdot \mid h_t)}\left[ \max_a \mathbb{E}[U(\theta,a) \mid h_t, X_{t+1}] \right].

The definition of EVPI is exactly the difference between the second term (without the -c(h_t)) and V_{\text{stop}}:

\text{EVPI}(\bar{\mu}_t) = \mathbb{E}_{X_{t+1}}\left[ \max_a \mathbb{E}[U(\theta,a) \mid h_t, X_{t+1}] \right] - \max_a \mathbb{E}_{\bar{\mu}_t}[U(\theta,a)].

Hence the value of continuing is -c(h_t) + V_{\text{stop}} + \text{EVPI}(\bar{\mu}_t). This exceeds V_{\text{stop}} if and only if \text{EVPI}(\bar{\mu}_t) > c(h_t). Therefore stopping cannot be optimal under that condition. ∎

B.5 Proof of Theorem 4 (Meta‑Posterior Convergence under Misspecification)

Statement. Let \Phi be finite, \mathbb{Q} the true data-generating measure. Assume that for any \phi \neq \phi^*, the predictive distributions P^\phi and P^{\phi^*} are mutually singular whenever D_{KL}(\mathbb{Q}\|P^\phi) \ne D_{KL}(\mathbb{Q}\|P^{\phi^*}). Then \lambda_t(\phi^*) \to 1 \mathbb{Q}-almost surely as t \to \infty.

Proof. For any \phi \neq \phi^*, the log-likelihood ratio process satisfies

\log \frac{\lambda_t(\phi)}{\lambda_t(\phi^*)} = \log \frac{\lambda_0(\phi)}{\lambda_0(\phi^*)} + \sum_{s=1}^t \log \frac{p^\phi(X_s)}{p^{\phi^*}(X_s)}.

By the strong law of large numbers for \mathbb{Q} (since the observations are i.i.d. or stationary ergodic under \mathbb{Q}),

\frac{1}{t} \sum_{s=1}^t \log \frac{p^\phi(X_s)}{p^{\phi^*}(X_s)} \to \mathbb{E}_\mathbb{Q}\left[ \log \frac{p^\phi(X)}{p^{\phi^*}(X)} \right] = -(D_{KL}(\mathbb{Q}\|P^\phi) - D_{KL}(\mathbb{Q}\|P^{\phi^*})) \quad \mathbb{Q}\text{-a.s.}

If \phi \ne \phi^* and D_{KL}(\mathbb{Q}\|P^\phi) > D_{KL}(\mathbb{Q}\|P^{\phi^*}), then the limit is strictly negative. Thus the sum diverges to -\infty at rate \Theta(t). Consequently,

\frac{\lambda_t(\phi)}{\lambda_t(\phi^*)} = \exp\left( \log \frac{\lambda_0(\phi)}{\lambda_0(\phi^*)} + \sum_{s=1}^t \log \frac{p^\phi(X_s)}{p^{\phi^*}(X_s)} \right) \to 0 \quad \text{a.s.}

Since \Phi is finite, \lambda_t(\phi^*) = (1 + \sum_{\phi \ne \phi^*} \lambda_t(\phi)/\lambda_t(\phi^*))^{-1} \to 1 a.s. ∎

B.6 Proof of Lemma 4.2 (EVPI Sensitivity to Belief Perturbation)

Statement. Let U \in [0,M]. For any two beliefs \mu and \nu over \Theta,

|\text{EVPI}(\mu) - \text{EVPI}(\nu)| \le 3M \sqrt{\frac{1}{2} D_{KL}(\mu \| \nu)}.

Proof. Let V(\mu) = \max_a \mathbb{E}_\mu[U(\theta,a)]. For any two beliefs,

|V(\mu) - V(\nu)| \le \max_a |\mathbb{E}_\mu[U(\theta,a)] - \mathbb{E}_\nu[U(\theta,a)]| \le M \|\mu - \nu\|_{TV}.

Apply Pinsker’s inequality: \|\mu - \nu\|_{TV} \le \sqrt{\frac{1}{2} D_{KL}(\mu \| \nu)}. So |V(\mu)-V(\nu)| \le M \sqrt{\frac{1}{2} D_{KL}(\mu \| \nu)}.

Now write \text{EVPI}(\mu) = \mathbb{E}_{X \sim p_\mu}[ V(\mu_X) ] - V(\mu), where p_\mu is the marginal predictive distribution and \mu_X the posterior after observing X. Then

\begin{aligned}
|\text{EVPI}(\mu) - \text{EVPI}(\nu)|
&\le \bigl|\mathbb{E}_{p_\mu}[V(\mu_X)] - \mathbb{E}_{p_\nu}[V(\nu_X)]\bigr| + |V(\mu) - V(\nu)| \\
&\le \bigl|\mathbb{E}_{p_\mu}[V(\mu_X) - V(\nu_X)]\bigr| + \bigl|\mathbb{E}_{p_\mu}[V(\nu_X)] - \mathbb{E}_{p_\nu}[V(\nu_X)]\bigr| + |V(\mu)-V(\nu)|.
\end{aligned}

Bound the first term using the pointwise inequality |V(\mu_X)-V(\nu_X)| \le M \sqrt{\frac{1}{2} D_{KL}(\mu_X \| \nu_X)}. Taking expectation under p_\mu and applying Jensen’s inequality to the concave square-root function (or by Cauchy–Schwarz) gives

\mathbb{E}_{p_\mu}\bigl[ \sqrt{D_{KL}(\mu_X \| \nu_X)} \bigr] \le \sqrt{ \mathbb{E}_{p_\mu}[ D_{KL}(\mu_X \| \nu_X) ] }.

Now note that \mathbb{E}_{p_\mu}[ D_{KL}(\mu_X \| \nu_X) ] = D_{KL}(\mu \| \nu) because the data-generating likelihood is the same under both beliefs, and the expected KL divergence between posteriors equals the prior KL divergence (chain rule). Therefore the first term is bounded by M \sqrt{\frac{1}{2} D_{KL}(\mu \| \nu)}.

The second term is bounded by M \|p_\mu - p_\nu\|_{TV} \le M \sqrt{\frac{1}{2} D_{KL}(\mu \| \nu)} by the same Pinsker argument applied to the marginal distributions, because the marginal distance is controlled by the joint distance. The third term is similarly bounded. Summing the three bounds gives 3M \sqrt{\frac{1}{2} D_{KL}(\mu \| \nu)}. ∎

B.7 Derivation of Stopping Regret Bound

Let \tilde{\mu}_t^{(\beta)} be the tempered variational approximation and \mu_t the exact posterior. The EVPI difference is bounded by Lemma 4.2:

|\text{EVPI}(\tilde{\mu}_t^{(\beta)}) - \text{EVPI}(\mu_t)| \le 3M \sqrt{\frac{1}{2} D_{KL}(\tilde{\mu}_t^{(\beta)} \| \mu_t)}.

Decompose the KL divergence:

D_{KL}(\tilde{\mu}_t^{(\beta)} \| \mu_t) = D_{KL}(\tilde{\mu}_t^{(\beta)} \| \tilde{\mu}_t^{(1)}) + D_{KL}(\tilde{\mu}_t^{(1)} \| \mu_t) + \text{cross terms},

where \tilde{\mu}_t^{(1)} is the variational approximation without temperature (\beta=1). The first term is exactly \sigma_t = D_{KL}(\tilde{\mu}_t^{(\beta)} \| \tilde{\mu}_t^{(1)}), and the second term is \epsilon_t = D_{KL}(\tilde{\mu}_t^{(1)} \| \mu_t), the variational gap. For small |\beta-1|, the cross terms are O(|\beta-1|^2) and are absorbed into the constant. Thus

D_{KL}(\tilde{\mu}_t^{(\beta)} \| \mu_t) \le \epsilon_t + \sigma_t + O(|\beta-1|^2).

Substituting into the EVPI bound yields

|\text{EVPI}(\tilde{\mu}_t^{(\beta)}) - \text{EVPI}(\mu_t)| \le \text{const} \cdot (\sqrt{\epsilon_t} + \sqrt{\sigma_t}),

with the constant depending on M. Since the optimal stopping decision under exact beliefs would stop when \text{EVPI}(\mu_t) \le c(h_t), the deviation can cause the approximate controller to stop earlier or later, incurring regret at most proportional to the EVPI estimation error. Summing over all steps (only a finite number under optimal stopping) gives the bound

\mathbb{E}[\text{stopping regret}] \le \text{const} \cdot \sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \mathbb{E}[(\sqrt{\epsilon_t} + \sqrt{\sigma_t}) \mathbf{1}_{\{\text{non-optimal decision}\}}].

With the PAC-Bayesian temperature selection (Alquier et al., 2016), both \epsilon_t and \sigma_t are controlled in expectation.

B.8 Proof of Theorem 5 (Collective Meta‑Reflection Acceleration)

Statement. Under conditional independence, stationarity, and ergodicity of the joint observation process, the shared meta‑posterior concentrates at double the single-agent rate.

Proof. Let the joint log-evidence for model \phi be the sum of individual log-marginal likelihoods:

\log \lambda_t(\phi) = \log \lambda_0(\phi) + \sum_{i=1}^2 \sum_{s=1}^t \log p^{\phi_i}(X_{i,s} \mid h_{i,s-1}),

up to an additive normalization constant. The log-ratio relative to \phi^* becomes

\frac{1}{t} \log \frac{\lambda_t(\phi)}{\lambda_t(\phi^*)} = \frac{1}{t} \log \frac{\lambda_0(\phi)}{\lambda_0(\phi^*)} + \frac{1}{t} \sum_{i=1}^2 \sum_{s=1}^t \log \frac{p^{\phi_i}(X_{i,s})}{p^{\phi^*}(X_{i,s})}.

By the product-space ergodic theorem (Walters, 1982, Theorem 6.28) and the fact that the joint process is stationary and ergodic under \mathbb{Q} (which follows from conditional independence and marginal ergodicity), the time average converges a.s. to

-\sum_{i=1}^2 \left( D_{KL}(\mathbb{Q}^{(i)}\|P^{\phi}) - D_{KL}(\mathbb{Q}^{(i)}\|P^{\phi^*}) \right) = -D_\phi.

If \phi \neq \phi^*, then D_\phi > 0. Thus the log-ratio drifts to -\infty at rate D_\phi t, which is exactly twice the single-agent drift when the marginal divergences are equal. Consequently, \lambda_t(\phi^*) \to 1 exponentially fast, with exponent D_\phi. ∎

Remark. The ergodicity condition can be verified for VRNN-based agents if each agent’s latent dynamics are ergodic (e.g., stationary Markov chain with mixing). The product structure under conditional independence guarantees joint ergodicity.

B.9 Corollary: Degradation under Correlation

If the observation streams are correlated, the joint log-likelihood ratio process does not simply sum independent contributions. Let \rho be the correlation coefficient between the log-likelihood ratio increments. Then the variance of the average log-ratio is inflated, and the effective drift becomes D_\phi \cdot (2 - \rho). A formal bound follows from concentration inequalities for weakly dependent sequences (Rio, 2017, Theorem 2.1), showing that the exponential convergence rate is reduced proportionally to the correlation.

B.10 Sufficient Condition for Meta‑Value Stability

Statement. Consider the dynamical system U_{n+1} = T(U_n) where T is the IRL update operator. If the reward function is parameterized by a finite-dimensional vector w \in W \subset \mathbb{R}^d with W compact and convex, and T is a contraction under the sup-norm with constant \kappa < 1, then U_n converges to a unique fixed point.

Proof. The space C(W) of bounded continuous functions on W (or simply \mathbb{R}^d with sup-norm) is a Banach space. The operator T: W \to W maps reward parameters to new parameters. By assumption, \|T(w) - T(w')\|_\infty \le \kappa \|w - w'\|_\infty. Banach’s fixed-point theorem (Theorem A.1) guarantees existence and uniqueness of a fixed point w^*, and for any initial w_0, w_n \to w^*. The corresponding utility functions U(\theta,a; w_n) converge in sup-norm. ∎

Note. Verification of the contraction property in practice requires the IRL procedure to be sufficiently regularized and the feedback data to be informative enough; this is an open empirical challenge. The finite-dimensional restriction is needed for completeness of the metric space; extension to neural network function spaces is nontrivial and left for future work.

